<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Black Rain — the ash storm that follows and extinguishes a large fire.

    THE TRIGGER IS 100% VANILLA, ZERO NEW CODE (RimSage,
    Source/RimWorld/WeatherDecider.cs, verified 2026-09-01):

    - Map.fireWatcher (Source/RimWorld/FireWatcher.cs) already tracks a
      running `fireDanger` off every live Fire thing's fireSize and exposes
      `LargeFireDangerPresent` once fireDanger > 90 — vanilla's own
      "this is a BIG fire" flag, ticked every 426 ticks, no new state needed.
    - WeatherDecider.CurrentWeatherCommonality (~line 185): "if
      (map.fireWatcher.LargeFireDangerPresent && weather.rainRate > 0.1f)
      num2 *= 15f;" — ANY weather with rainRate > 0.1 gets its commonality
      multiplied FIFTEEN-FOLD the instant a large fire is burning (vanilla's
      own existing ChanceFactorRainOnFire mechanic). Black Rain only needs
      rainRate set and ordinary membership in the biome's weather table
      (PyrelandsWeather_BlackRain.xml, RimUtinni: Fire Ecology) to inherit
      that for free.
    - WeatherDeciderTick (~line 71): LargeFireDangerPresent ALSO shrinks the
      weather-check interval to 25% of normal, so the roll happens ~4x more
      often while the fire is large — the switch arrives quickly once the
      fire is big enough, not on the next lazy roll.
    - Extinguishing is equally vanilla: Fire.TickInterval (Source/RimWorld/
      Fire.cs:~309) already applies Extinguish damage to any rain-vulnerable
      fire whenever map.weatherManager.RainRate > 0.01 — nothing reads Black
      Rain specially, it "rains" and vanilla's own fire code does the rest.

    No eventMakers on this one, deliberately — DryThunderstorm/
    RainyThunderstorm both spawn WeatherEvent_LightningStrike; Black Rain is
    the loop's full stop, not a fresh ignition source.

    Kept rare outside a big fire (a low base baseWeatherCommonalities share)
    so it reads as "the one time it rains" per R-H1/R-H2, not an ordinary
    roll — see PyrelandsWeather_BlackRain.xml for the number.
  -->

  <WeatherDef>
    <defName>RSW_FE_BlackRain</defName>
    <label>black rain</label>
    <description>A short, filthy downpour of ash-laden condensate, dragged out of a fire's own convection column. It blackens everything it touches, cuts visibility and accuracy, and puts out the fire it came from — the one time it truly rains here.</description>
    <ambientSounds>
      <li>Ambient_Rain</li>
    </ambientSounds>
    <durationRange>2500~6000</durationRange>
    <favorability>VeryBad</favorability>
    <isBad>true</isBad>
    <accuracyMultiplier>0.55</accuracyMultiplier>
    <moveSpeedMultiplier>0.85</moveSpeedMultiplier>
    <perceivePriority>3</perceivePriority>
    <rainRate>1.1</rainRate>
    <overlayClasses>
      <li>WeatherOverlay_Rain</li>
      <li>WeatherOverlay_Fog</li>
    </overlayClasses>
    <skyColorsDay>
      <sky>(0.22,0.20,0.20)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.5,0.5,0.5)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.3,0.28,0.28)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.4</saturation>
    </skyColorsDay>
    <skyColorsDusk>
      <sky>(0.26,0.22,0.22)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.5,0.5,0.5)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.25,0.22,0.22)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.4</saturation>
    </skyColorsDusk>
    <skyColorsNightEdge>
      <sky>(0.12,0.11,0.11)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.4,0.4,0.4)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.18,0.16,0.16)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.4</saturation>
    </skyColorsNightEdge>
    <skyColorsNightMid>
      <sky>(0.12,0.11,0.11)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.4,0.4,0.4)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.18,0.16,0.16)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.4</saturation>
    </skyColorsNightMid>
    <temperatureRange>-40~999</temperatureRange>
    <weatherThought>SoakingWet</weatherThought>
    <windSpeedFactor>1.3</windSpeedFactor>
    <windSpeedOffset>1.0</windSpeedOffset>
  </WeatherDef>

</Defs>
